window.PARK_DATA={"catalogue":{"people":[{"id":"euclid","name":"Euclid","born":-325,"field":"geometry","title":"A language for mathematical proof","bio":"Working in ancient Alexandria, Euclid organized geometry and number theory into the Elements. Definitions, postulates and deductions became an enduring model of mathematical exposition.","formula":"a² + b² = c²","impact":"An axiomatic theory distinguishes its assumptions from what follows logically. Euclid's architecture of proof became an intellectual reference point for later geometry and foundations.","questions":["What is the simplest example of a language for mathematical proof?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Euclid/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"archimedes","name":"Archimedes","born":-287,"field":"analysis","title":"Measuring the curved world","bio":"Archimedes combined geometry, mechanics and ingenious limiting arguments. He found exact areas and volumes and obtained rigorous bounds on π.","formula":"A = πr²","impact":"His method of exhaustion traps a curved quantity between simpler approximations. This is an important historical predecessor of integral calculus, not calculus in its modern formulation.","questions":["What is the simplest example of measuring the curved world?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Archimedes/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"diophantus","name":"Diophantus","born":200,"field":"algebra","title":"Equations with arithmetic constraints","bio":"Diophantus's Arithmetica studies numerical problems through equations, often seeking rational solutions. His name became attached to equations whose solutions are constrained to integers.","formula":"x² + y² = z²","impact":"Asking for integer or rational solutions changes an algebraic problem completely. This arithmetic perspective ultimately reaches modern number theory and questions of decidability.","questions":["What is the simplest example of equations with arithmetic constraints?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Diophantus/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"aryabhata","name":"Aryabhata","born":476,"field":"algebra","title":"Algorithms for astronomy and arithmetic","bio":"Aryabhata composed the Aryabhatiya, combining astronomy with computational mathematics. It includes methods for numerical calculation, trigonometry and indeterminate equations.","formula":"ax + by = c","impact":"His work illustrates an early algorithmic culture: a mathematical method can be specified as a sequence of reliable computational steps.","questions":["What is the simplest example of algorithms for astronomy and arithmetic?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Aryabhata_I/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"al-khwarizmi","name":"Al-Khwarizmi","born":780,"field":"algebra","title":"Systematic algebra","bio":"Al-Khwarizmi worked in the mathematical culture of Abbasid Baghdad. His algebraic treatise systematized the solution of linear and quadratic equations, and his arithmetic writings helped transmit positional calculation.","formula":"x² + bx = c","impact":"The words algebra and algorithm reflect the reception of his work. His equations were expressed rhetorically rather than in today's symbolic notation.","questions":["What is the simplest example of systematic algebra?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Al-Khwarizmi/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"khayyam","name":"Omar Khayyam","born":1048,"field":"algebra","title":"Cubic equations meet geometry","bio":"Omar Khayyam was a mathematician, astronomer and poet. He classified cubic equations and treated many of them geometrically through intersections of conic sections.","formula":"y = x²,  x² + y² = r²","impact":"Curves can encode algebraic relations, and an intersection can represent a solution. His work predates the fully developed coordinate geometry of Descartes.","questions":["What is the simplest example of cubic equations meet geometry?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Khayyam/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"fibonacci","name":"Fibonacci","born":1170,"field":"algebra","title":"Arithmetic travels across cultures","bio":"Leonardo of Pisa, known as Fibonacci, promoted Hindu–Arabic arithmetic in Latin Europe through Liber abaci. Its problems connect practical calculation with number theory.","formula":"Fₙ₊₂ = Fₙ₊₁ + Fₙ","impact":"A simple recurrence produces a rich mathematical sequence. The rabbit problem is only a small part of his much broader contribution to computation and arithmetic.","questions":["What is the simplest example of arithmetic travels across cultures?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Fibonacci/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"descartes","name":"René Descartes","born":1596,"field":"geometry","title":"Geometry in coordinates","bio":"Descartes helped develop analytic geometry by translating geometric problems into equations. His work made symbolic algebra a systematic instrument for studying curves.","formula":"x² + y² = r²","impact":"Coordinates allow geometric questions to become algebraic calculations. This bridge underlies much of calculus, linear algebra and mathematical modeling.","questions":["What is the simplest example of geometry in coordinates?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Descartes/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"fermat","name":"Pierre de Fermat","born":1607,"field":"algebra","title":"Arithmetic problems with long afterlives","bio":"Fermat contributed to number theory, analytic geometry, optimization and probability. Many of his assertions became starting points for generations of later mathematics.","formula":"aᵖ ≡ a (mod p)","impact":"For prime p, this congruence is a basic tool of modular arithmetic. His work helped turn patterns among integers into a sustained theoretical program.","questions":["What is the simplest example of arithmetic problems with long afterlives?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Fermat/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"pascal","name":"Blaise Pascal","born":1623,"field":"analysis","title":"Counting the uncertain","bio":"Pascal worked on geometry, calculation and probability. His correspondence with Fermat helped develop mathematical reasoning about games of chance.","formula":"C(n,k) = C(n−1,k−1) + C(n−1,k)","impact":"Pascal's triangle organizes binomial coefficients. The same recurrence connects counting, probability and algebraic expansion.","questions":["What is the simplest example of counting the uncertain?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Pascal/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"newton","name":"Isaac Newton","born":1643,"field":"analysis","title":"Change becomes calculable","bio":"Newton developed calculus and used mathematics to formulate laws of motion and gravitation. His mathematical physics showed how differential equations can describe a changing world.","formula":"F = ma","impact":"Rates of change and accumulated quantities became calculable parts of a physical theory. Calculus was developed independently by Leibniz in a different notation.","questions":["What is the simplest example of change becomes calculable?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Newton/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"leibniz","name":"G. W. Leibniz","born":1646,"field":"logic","title":"A calculus of symbols","bio":"Leibniz independently developed calculus and advocated systematic symbolic reasoning. His differential and integral notation became particularly influential.","formula":"∫ f′(x) dx = f(x) + C","impact":"A good symbolic language can make transformations easier to discover and communicate. His wider logical ambitions anticipated later formal approaches without already constituting modern computation theory.","questions":["What is the simplest example of a calculus of symbols?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Leibniz/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"euler","name":"Leonhard Euler","born":1707,"field":"analysis","title":"Connections across mathematics","bio":"Euler worked across analysis, number theory, geometry, mechanics and combinatorics. His solution of the Königsberg bridge problem is a foundational episode in graph theory.","formula":"eⁱπ + 1 = 0","impact":"Euler repeatedly discovered that distinct mathematical languages describe the same structure. His identity links exponential functions, complex numbers and trigonometry.","questions":["What is the simplest example of connections across mathematics?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Euler/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"lagrange","name":"Joseph-Louis Lagrange","born":1736,"field":"analysis","title":"Optimization through structure","bio":"Lagrange developed analytical mechanics and the calculus of variations. His multiplier method incorporates equality constraints into optimization.","formula":"∇f = λ∇g","impact":"At a regular constrained optimum, the objective cannot change in a feasible tangent direction. This observation becomes a systematic method for solving constrained problems.","questions":["What is the simplest example of optimization through structure?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lagrange/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"laplace","name":"Pierre-Simon Laplace","born":1749,"field":"analysis","title":"Probability as a mathematical theory","bio":"Laplace made major contributions to celestial mechanics, probability and analysis. He systematized probabilistic methods for inference and approximation.","formula":"Δf = 0","impact":"The Laplace equation links equilibrium, potential theory and geometry. His probability work helped make uncertainty a subject of rigorous quantitative analysis.","questions":["What is the simplest example of probability as a mathematical theory?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Laplace/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"fourier","name":"Joseph Fourier","born":1768,"field":"analysis","title":"Decompose a signal into frequencies","bio":"Fourier studied heat flow using expansions into trigonometric components. Fourier analysis became a central way of understanding functions and differential equations.","formula":"f(x) ≈ Σₖ aₖ cos(kx) + bₖ sin(kx)","impact":"A complicated function can be studied through simpler oscillations. The conditions and meaning of convergence matter, and became major questions in analysis.","questions":["What is the simplest example of decompose a signal into frequencies?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Fourier/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"gauss","name":"Carl Friedrich Gauss","born":1777,"field":"algebra","title":"Arithmetic, geometry and precision","bio":"Gauss shaped number theory, geometry, statistics and mathematical astronomy. Disquisitiones Arithmeticae placed congruences and quadratic forms at the center of arithmetic.","formula":"a ≡ b (mod m)","impact":"Modular arithmetic isolates the structure of remainders. Gauss's work gave later number theory both foundational results and a standard of conceptual precision.","questions":["What is the simplest example of arithmetic, geometry and precision?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Gauss/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"cauchy","name":"Augustin-Louis Cauchy","born":1789,"field":"analysis","title":"Rigorous analysis","bio":"Cauchy helped reorganize analysis around precise concepts of convergence and continuity. He also developed central parts of complex analysis.","formula":"f(z) = (1 / 2πi) ∮ f(ζ)/(ζ−z) dζ","impact":"Under the Cauchy integral formula's hypotheses, values inside a contour are determined by boundary data. Holomorphic functions consequently have extraordinary rigidity.","questions":["What is the simplest example of rigorous analysis?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Cauchy/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"galois","name":"Évariste Galois","born":1811,"field":"algebra","title":"Symmetry explains solvability","bio":"Galois related polynomial equations to permutation groups of their roots. His work transformed the question of solving equations by radicals into a structural question about groups.","formula":"Gal(L/K)","impact":"A polynomial's hidden symmetries govern which algebraic operations can recover its roots. The approach made group theory central to algebra.","questions":["What is the simplest example of symmetry explains solvability?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Galois/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"boole","name":"George Boole","born":1815,"field":"logic","title":"Logic becomes algebra","bio":"Boole introduced an algebraic treatment of logical reasoning. His work helped establish the idea that logical operations can be manipulated symbolically.","formula":"x ∧ y,  x ∨ y,  ¬x","impact":"Boolean algebra eventually became a foundation of digital circuit design. Shannon supplied the explicit engineering bridge to switching circuits.","questions":["What is the simplest example of logic becomes algebra?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Boole/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"lovelace","name":"Ada Lovelace","born":1815,"field":"systems","title":"Programs before electronic computers","bio":"Lovelace's notes on Babbage's Analytical Engine included a detailed scheme for computing Bernoulli numbers. She also considered how a general symbolic machine might manipulate more than numerical quantities.","formula":"instructions + data → computation","impact":"Her importance lies in the conception and explanation of programmable symbolic machinery. The Analytical Engine itself was not completed in her lifetime.","questions":["What is the simplest example of programs before electronic computers?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lovelace/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"cayley","name":"Arthur Cayley","born":1821,"field":"algebra","title":"Algebraic structures in their own right","bio":"Cayley developed matrix theory and abstract group theory. He also contributed to invariant theory and the enumeration of combinatorial structures.","formula":"G ↪ S_G","impact":"Every group acts faithfully on itself by left multiplication. Cayley's theorem makes an abstract group concrete as a group of permutations.","questions":["What is the simplest example of algebraic structures in their own right?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Cayley/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"riemann","name":"Bernhard Riemann","born":1826,"field":"geometry","title":"Geometry beyond flat space","bio":"Riemann introduced a flexible framework for curved spaces and made profound contributions to complex analysis and number theory. His 1854 geometry lecture changed the meaning of space in mathematics.","formula":"ds² = Σᵢⱼ gᵢⱼ dxᵢ dxⱼ","impact":"A metric can vary from point to point. This permits intrinsic geometry on manifolds and later became essential to general relativity.","questions":["What is the simplest example of geometry beyond flat space?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Riemann/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"dedekind","name":"Richard Dedekind","born":1831,"field":"algebra","title":"Numbers defined by structure","bio":"Dedekind developed structural foundations for the real numbers and algebraic number theory. Ideals provide a replacement for unique factorization of elements in settings where it fails.","formula":"ℝ via Dedekind cuts","impact":"A real number can be described through a partition of the rationals. Structural definitions clarify what an object is without depending on a particular numerical approximation.","questions":["What is the simplest example of numbers defined by structure?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Dedekind/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"cantor","name":"Georg Cantor","born":1845,"field":"logic","title":"Different sizes of infinity","bio":"Cantor founded set theory and distinguished countable from uncountable infinity. His diagonal argument demonstrates limits on attempts to enumerate all infinite binary sequences.","formula":"|ℕ| < |ℝ|","impact":"Infinity is not one undifferentiated size. Diagonalization also became a recurring tool in logic and computability.","questions":["What is the simplest example of different sizes of infinity?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Cantor/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"frege","name":"Gottlob Frege","born":1848,"field":"logic","title":"Quantifiers and formal reasoning","bio":"Frege developed a powerful formal notation for logic in his Begriffsschrift. His work brought quantification and the structure of mathematical inference into a precise symbolic system.","formula":"∀x ∃y R(x,y)","impact":"Modern predicate logic owes much to the distinction between objects, functions and quantified assertions. Frege's later foundational system also exposed the dangers of unrestricted comprehension.","questions":["What is the simplest example of quantifiers and formal reasoning?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Frege/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"poincare","name":"Henri Poincaré","born":1854,"field":"geometry","title":"Shape and dynamics","bio":"Poincaré helped create algebraic topology and qualitative dynamical systems. He studied global structure rather than relying only on explicit solutions of equations.","formula":"π₁(X)","impact":"The fundamental group records how loops can wind through a space. It translates a geometric problem into an algebraic invariant.","questions":["What is the simplest example of shape and dynamics?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Poincare/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"markov","name":"Andrey Markov","born":1856,"field":"analysis","title":"Randomness with memory of the present","bio":"Markov studied stochastic processes whose next state depends on the present state. These processes became a basic model of dependent randomness.","formula":"Pr(Xₜ₊₁=j ∣ Xₜ=i) = Pᵢⱼ","impact":"A transition matrix turns long-term random behavior into a problem in probability and linear algebra. Markov chains are central to sampling and randomized algorithms.","questions":["What is the simplest example of randomness with memory of the present?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Markov/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"peano","name":"Giuseppe Peano","born":1858,"field":"logic","title":"Arithmetic from axioms","bio":"Peano developed an influential axiomatization and notation for arithmetic. He also constructed a continuous space-filling curve, challenging geometric intuition.","formula":"0, S(0), S(S(0)), …","impact":"A small collection of axioms can support systematic reasoning about numbers. First-order arithmetic nevertheless has nonstandard models and is subject to incompleteness.","questions":["What is the simplest example of arithmetic from axioms?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Peano/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"hilbert","name":"David Hilbert","born":1862,"field":"logic","title":"Certificates, axioms and algebra","bio":"Hilbert transformed invariant theory, algebraic geometry and mathematical foundations. His work linked finite generation, algebraic certificates and ambitious programs for formal proof.","formula":"Σᵢ gᵢfᵢ = 1","impact":"Over an algebraically closed field, a finite polynomial system with no common zero admits this Nullstellensatz certificate. Existence of a certificate does not guarantee that it is small.","questions":["How does a CNF clause become a polynomial equation?","What is the difference between certificate degree and size?","Why does finite generation not imply efficient decidability?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Hilbert/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"russell","name":"Bertrand Russell","born":1872,"field":"logic","title":"A paradox at the foundations","bio":"Russell's paradox showed that unrestricted set formation is inconsistent. With Whitehead, he pursued a systematic logical foundation for mathematics in Principia Mathematica.","formula":"R = {x : x ∉ x}","impact":"Asking whether R contains itself gives a contradiction. The lesson is about restrictions on a formal system, not an inconsistency of every theory of sets.","questions":["What is the simplest example of a paradox at the foundations?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Russell/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"lebesgue","name":"Henri Lebesgue","born":1875,"field":"analysis","title":"A more flexible integral","bio":"Lebesgue developed measure and integration that accommodate limits and irregular functions far better than the classical Riemann framework.","formula":"∫ f dμ","impact":"Measuring the sizes of level sets offers a powerful way to integrate. The resulting framework supports modern probability and functional analysis.","questions":["What is the simplest example of a more flexible integral?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lebesgue/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"hardy","name":"G. H. Hardy","born":1877,"field":"algebra","title":"Analysis in the service of numbers","bio":"Hardy worked in analysis and analytic number theory, notably with Littlewood and Ramanujan. Their methods brought estimates, asymptotics and complex analysis to arithmetic questions.","formula":"p(n) ∼ e^{π√(2n/3)} / (4n√3)","impact":"The Hardy–Ramanujan asymptotic estimates the number of integer partitions. It exemplifies how analysis can reveal the growth of a discrete counting function.","questions":["What is the simplest example of analysis in the service of numbers?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Hardy/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"brouwer","name":"L. E. J. Brouwer","born":1881,"field":"geometry","title":"A point that cannot move","bio":"Brouwer proved foundational results in topology, including his fixed-point theorem. He also developed intuitionism, a distinct philosophy of mathematical proof.","formula":"f : Bⁿ → Bⁿ ⇒ ∃x, f(x)=x","impact":"Every continuous self-map of a closed Euclidean ball has a fixed point. This existence theorem became central to equilibrium theory and computational fixed-point problems.","questions":["What is the simplest example of a point that cannot move?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Brouwer/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"noether","name":"Emmy Noether","born":1882,"field":"algebra","title":"Structure and symmetry","bio":"Noether reshaped abstract algebra through structural methods and established a fundamental relation between continuous symmetries and conservation laws.","formula":"symmetry → conservation law","impact":"Noether's theorem applies under precise variational hypotheses. Her separate algebraic work made rings, ideals and modules central organizing objects.","questions":["What is the simplest example of structure and symmetry?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Noether_Emmy/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"ramanujan","name":"Srinivasa Ramanujan","born":1887,"field":"algebra","title":"Unexpected arithmetic patterns","bio":"Ramanujan made extraordinary contributions to partitions, series, continued fractions and modular forms. His collaboration with Hardy connected his discoveries to analytic number theory.","formula":"p(5n+4) ≡ 0 (mod 5)","impact":"A simple-looking congruence reflects deep structure in generating functions. His notebooks continued to generate mathematical work long after his death.","questions":["What is the simplest example of unexpected arithmetic patterns?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Ramanujan/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"banach","name":"Stefan Banach","born":1892,"field":"analysis","title":"Infinite-dimensional geometry","bio":"Banach helped establish functional analysis as a systematic subject. Complete normed vector spaces allow functions themselves to be treated as geometric points.","formula":"∥T(x)−T(y)∥ ≤ c∥x−y∥, c<1","impact":"A contraction on a complete metric space has a unique fixed point. Repeated iteration converges to it, connecting existence with an explicit method.","questions":["What is the simplest example of infinite-dimensional geometry?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Banach/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"wiener","name":"Norbert Wiener","born":1894,"field":"information","title":"Feedback, noise and control","bio":"Wiener worked on probability, harmonic analysis and cybernetics. He treated communication and control as systems involving noise, prediction and feedback.","formula":"output → feedback → input","impact":"Feedback changes a system in response to its observed behavior. His work connected rigorous stochastic analysis with engineering and the study of adaptive systems.","questions":["What is the simplest example of feedback, noise and control?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Wiener/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"post","name":"Emil Post","born":1897,"field":"complexity","title":"Computation through rewriting","bio":"Post developed formal production systems, work on recursive enumerability, and the Post correspondence problem. His approaches helped map the limits of effective procedures.","formula":"uᵢ₁uᵢ₂…uᵢₖ = vᵢ₁vᵢ₂…vᵢₖ","impact":"The Post correspondence problem asks whether tiles can be matched into equal strings. Its undecidability provides a powerful source problem for reductions.","questions":["What is the simplest example of computation through rewriting?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Post/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"tarski","name":"Alfred Tarski","born":1901,"field":"logic","title":"Truth, models and decidability","bio":"Tarski gave a mathematical account of truth in formal languages and contributed to model theory and algebraic logic. He established decidability for the first-order theory of real closed fields.","formula":"T ⊨ φ","impact":"Semantic consequence means truth in every model of the axioms. The connection between syntax and semantics became fundamental to logic.","questions":["What is the simplest example of truth, models and decidability?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Tarski/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"kolmogorov","name":"Andrey Kolmogorov","born":1903,"field":"analysis","title":"Probability from axioms","bio":"Kolmogorov formulated the measure-theoretic axioms of probability and later developed major ideas in randomness and algorithmic information.","formula":"Pr(⋃ᵢ Aᵢ) = Σᵢ Pr(Aᵢ)","impact":"For pairwise disjoint events, countable additivity organizes probability into measure theory. His separate complexity notion measures the length of the shortest effective description.","questions":["What is the simplest example of probability from axioms?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Kolmogorov/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"church","name":"Alonzo Church","born":1903,"field":"complexity","title":"Functions as computation","bio":"Church introduced lambda calculus and proved an undecidability result for first-order logic. Lambda calculus became a foundational model of computation and programming-language semantics.","formula":"(λx.M) N → M[x:=N]","impact":"Computation can be expressed through substitution and function application. Different formal models agreeing on computability helped motivate the Church–Turing thesis.","questions":["What is the simplest example of functions as computation?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Church/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"von-neumann","name":"John von Neumann","born":1903,"field":"complexity","title":"Mathematics for machines and games","bio":"Von Neumann contributed to game theory, operator algebras, quantum foundations and computing. His EDVAC report was an influential account of stored-program design developed in a broader collaborative setting.","formula":"maxₓ minᵧ xᵀAy = minᵧ maxₓ xᵀAy","impact":"The minimax theorem gives finite two-player zero-sum games a common value when mixed strategies are allowed. It connects strategic choice with convex optimization.","questions":["Why does randomization make minimax true?","How does a zero-sum game become a linear program?","What is the mathematical role of an operator algebra?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Von_Neumann/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"godel","name":"Kurt Gödel","born":1906,"field":"logic","title":"The limits of formal proof","bio":"Gödel proved the completeness theorem for first-order logic and the incompleteness theorems for effective arithmetic theories. He also developed the constructible universe in set theory.","formula":"T ⊨ φ ⇔ T ⊢ φ","impact":"Logical completeness concerns truth in all models. Arithmetic incompleteness concerns what a particular effectively axiomatized consistent theory can decide.","questions":["How does arithmetic encode a proof?","Why do nonstandard models reconcile completeness and incompleteness?","How do Gödel’s and Turing’s diagonal arguments differ?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Godel/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"hopper","name":"Grace Hopper","born":1906,"field":"systems","title":"From machine instructions to languages","bio":"Hopper worked on early computers, automatic programming and compiler development. She played an important role in the movement toward machine-independent business programming languages.","formula":"source program → translation → execution","impact":"Higher-level languages separate what a program expresses from one machine's instruction set. This made programming accessible at a much larger scale.","questions":["What is the simplest example of from machine instructions to languages?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Hopper/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"whitney","name":"Hassler Whitney","born":1907,"field":"graphs","title":"Independence beyond vectors","bio":"Whitney introduced matroids as a way to axiomatize independence, while also making foundational contributions to differential topology.","formula":"I,J independent, |I|<|J| ⇒ ∃e∈J∖I: I+e independent","impact":"The exchange axiom unifies linear independence with forests in graphs. This abstraction later became central to combinatorial optimization.","questions":["What is the simplest example of independence beyond vectors?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Whitney/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"kleene","name":"Stephen Kleene","born":1909,"field":"logic","title":"The structure of effective computation","bio":"Kleene developed recursive function theory and major parts of mathematical logic. His work on regular events also helped establish the theory behind regular expressions.","formula":"L* = ⋃ₙ≥0 Lⁿ","impact":"Kleene star expresses arbitrary finite repetition. The equivalence of regular expressions and finite automata connects symbolic pattern descriptions with machines.","questions":["What is the simplest example of the structure of effective computation?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Kleene/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"ulam","name":"Stanislaw Ulam","born":1909,"field":"graphs","title":"Random sampling as a method","bio":"Ulam made contributions to set theory, analysis and combinatorics. With collaborators he helped develop Monte Carlo methods for numerical problems.","formula":"E[X] ≈ (X₁+⋯+Xₘ)/m","impact":"Sampling can estimate a quantity that is difficult to compute exactly. The error depends on the distribution, variance and sampling method, not merely on the sample count.","questions":["What is the simplest example of random sampling as a method?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Ulam/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"turing","name":"Alan Turing","born":1912,"field":"complexity","title":"A mathematical model of computation","bio":"Turing defined an abstract computing machine, established universal simulation and proved an undecidability result. His later work included cryptanalysis, machine intelligence and morphogenesis.","formula":"U(⟨M,x⟩) = M(x)","impact":"One machine can simulate the description of another. Encoding programs as data makes universality and diagonal arguments possible.","questions":["How is universal simulation encoded?","How can halting reduce to a concrete combinatorial problem?","Which statements about program behavior are decidable under restrictions?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Turing/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"erdos","name":"Paul Erdős","born":1913,"field":"graphs","title":"Existence through randomness","bio":"Erdős transformed combinatorics through the probabilistic method and developed random graph theory with Rényi. He created influential problems and collaborations across many areas.","formula":"E[X] < 1 ⇒ Pr(X=0) > 0","impact":"For a nonnegative integer-valued X, expectation below one forces an outcome with no counted defects. This turns a probability estimate into an existence proof.","questions":["How do you balance short-cycle counts against independence number?","When can conditional expectations derandomize an existence proof?","What changes when maximum degree is fixed in advance?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Erdos/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"dantzig","name":"George Dantzig","born":1914,"field":"algorithms","title":"Linear optimization at scale","bio":"Dantzig developed the simplex method for linear programming. The method and its applications helped establish operations research and computational optimization.","formula":"maximize cᵀx subject to Ax≤b","impact":"A linear program has geometric structure that can be exploited algorithmically. Simplex is highly influential despite exponential worst-case examples.","questions":["What is the simplest example of linear optimization at scale?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Dantzig/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"shannon","name":"Claude Shannon","born":1916,"field":"information","title":"Information becomes a quantity","bio":"Shannon linked Boolean algebra to switching circuits and founded information theory. Entropy, channel capacity and coding theorems made communication limits mathematically precise.","formula":"H(X) = −Σₓ p(x) log₂ p(x)","impact":"Entropy measures average information under a distribution. For an independent identically distributed source, it controls asymptotic lossless compression rates.","questions":["How does typical-set compression work?","Why are zero-error and vanishing-error capacity different?","How can a graph encode confusable symbols?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Shannon/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"simon","name":"Herbert Simon","born":1916,"field":"information","title":"Intelligence as problem solving","bio":"Simon studied decision-making, bounded rationality and artificial intelligence. With Allen Newell he built influential models of heuristic problem solving.","formula":"limited resources → bounded rationality","impact":"A decision-maker may seek a satisfactory solution rather than solve an idealized global optimization problem. This connects cognitive modeling with computational constraints.","questions":["What is the simplest example of intelligence as problem solving?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"Computer science as empirical inquiry: Symbols and search · Newell and Simon","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/360018.360022","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · Herbert Simon","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/simon_1031467.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"tutte","name":"W. T. Tutte","born":1917,"field":"graphs","title":"The structure of matchings and flows","bio":"Tutte established foundational results in graph theory, including a characterization of graphs with perfect matchings. His work shaped chromatic and flow theory.","formula":"odd(G−S) ≤ |S| for every S⊆V(G)","impact":"A finite graph has a perfect matching exactly when every deleted vertex set leaves at most that many odd components. The condition reveals global obstructions to pairing.","questions":["What is the simplest example of the structure of matchings and flows?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Tutte/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"bellman","name":"Richard Bellman","born":1920,"field":"algorithms","title":"Solve once, reuse the answer","bio":"Bellman developed dynamic programming: organize a problem into overlapping subproblems whose optimal values satisfy a recurrence.","formula":"V(s) = maxₐ {r(s,a) + γ E[V(s′)]}","impact":"The displayed Bellman equation is a discounted decision-process form. The broader principle of optimality became central to optimization, control and algorithm design.","questions":["What is the simplest example of solve once, reuse the answer?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Bellman/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"codd","name":"Edgar F. Codd","born":1923,"field":"systems","title":"Data as relations","bio":"Codd proposed the relational model for databases. He separated logical data organization from low-level storage details.","formula":"σ, π, ⋈","impact":"Selection, projection and join describe data manipulation algebraically. The relational viewpoint reshaped database design and query languages.","questions":["What is the simplest example of data as relations?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"Relational database: A practical foundation for productivity · Codd","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/358396.358400","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · Edgar F. Codd","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/codd_1000892.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"backus","name":"John Backus","born":1924,"field":"systems","title":"High-level programming","bio":"Backus led the team that developed FORTRAN and helped shape programming-language notation. He later advocated function-level programming.","formula":"program meaning ≠ machine layout","impact":"An effective high-level language can express scientific computation while allowing compilation to efficient machine code. Language design itself became a serious theoretical discipline.","questions":["What is the simplest example of high-level programming?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"Can programming be liberated from the von Neumann style? · Backus","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/359576.359579","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · John Backus","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/backus_0703524.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"serre","name":"Jean-Pierre Serre","born":1926,"field":"geometry","title":"Topology meets algebraic geometry","bio":"Serre made foundational contributions to topology, algebraic geometry and number theory. His work brought powerful algebraic methods into the study of spaces.","formula":"Hᵏ(X,ℱ)","impact":"Cohomology records global information from local algebraic data. Serre's work helped build bridges among formerly distinct mathematical subjects.","questions":["What is the simplest example of topology meets algebraic geometry?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Serre/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"mccarthy","name":"John McCarthy","born":1927,"field":"information","title":"A language for symbolic intelligence","bio":"McCarthy helped establish artificial intelligence as a field and designed Lisp. He also worked on formalizing common-sense reasoning.","formula":"(f x)","impact":"Symbolic data and programs can be represented in closely related forms. Lisp made recursive symbolic computation an influential programming style.","questions":["What is the simplest example of a language for symbolic intelligence?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"John McCarthy · Stanford research archive","url":"https://www-formal.stanford.edu/jmc/","type":"research"}]},{"id":"minsky","name":"Marvin Minsky","born":1927,"field":"information","title":"Models of intelligence","bio":"Minsky helped shape early artificial intelligence through work on learning, representation and computation. With Papert, he studied the expressive limitations of perceptrons.","formula":"representation + search + learning","impact":"A computational theory of intelligence must explain both representation and procedure. His work influenced symbolic AI and debates about learning architectures.","questions":["What is the simplest example of models of intelligence?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"ACM · Marvin Minsky","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/minsky_7440781.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"newell","name":"Allen Newell","born":1927,"field":"information","title":"Heuristic search for reasoning","bio":"Newell, with Simon and collaborators, developed early programs for logical problem solving. He later worked on unified theories of cognition.","formula":"state → operator → new state","impact":"Problem solving can be organized as search through states. Heuristics guide the search without changing the distinction between finding a proof and checking it.","questions":["What is the simplest example of heuristic search for reasoning?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"Computer science as empirical inquiry: Symbols and search · Newell and Simon","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/360018.360022","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · Allen Newell","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/newell_3167755.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"nash","name":"John Nash","born":1928,"field":"analysis","title":"Equilibrium beyond zero-sum games","bio":"Nash proved the existence of mixed-strategy equilibria in finite games. He also made major contributions to geometry and partial differential equations.","formula":"uᵢ(sᵢ*,s₋ᵢ*) ≥ uᵢ(sᵢ,s₋ᵢ*)","impact":"No player can gain through a unilateral deviation. Equilibrium need not be unique, efficient or reached by a natural learning process.","questions":["How does the fixed-point map encode best responses?","What precisely makes END OF LINE a total search problem?","Why can a three-player equilibrium require irrational numbers?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Nash/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"grothendieck","name":"Alexander Grothendieck","born":1928,"field":"geometry","title":"A new language for geometry","bio":"Grothendieck rebuilt major parts of algebraic geometry around schemes, functors and cohomology. His methods allow geometric reasoning over rings as well as fields.","formula":"X = Spec(A)","impact":"A scheme retains information, including infinitesimal structure, that an ordinary set of solutions may lose. This framework transformed arithmetic geometry.","questions":["Why does a nilpotent element carry geometric information?","What does changing the base from Z to Fₚ do?","What do orbit closures mean in algebraic complexity?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Grothendieck/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"atiyah","name":"Michael Atiyah","born":1929,"field":"geometry","title":"Analysis detects topology","bio":"Atiyah helped develop topological K-theory and, with Isadore Singer, proved the index theorem. The result connects differential operators with topological data.","formula":"analytic index = topological index","impact":"An analytic count involving the kernel and cokernel of an elliptic operator can be computed from geometric-topological invariants.","questions":["What is the simplest example of analysis detects topology?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Atiyah/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"dijkstra","name":"Edsger Dijkstra","born":1930,"field":"algorithms","title":"Algorithms with invariants","bio":"Dijkstra developed influential algorithms and methods for structured programming and correctness. His shortest-path algorithm is a model of greedy reasoning.","formula":"dist(v) = minᵤ {dist(u)+w(u,v)}","impact":"With nonnegative edge weights, the closest unsettled vertex can be finalized safely. The proof invariant is as important as the implementation.","questions":["What is the simplest example of algorithms with invariants?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Dijkstra/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"rabin","name":"Michael Rabin","born":1931,"field":"complexity","title":"Randomness as an algorithmic resource","bio":"Rabin contributed to automata theory, logic, randomized algorithms and cryptography. His primality test made probabilistic computation a practical mathematical tool.","formula":"random test + repetition → controlled error","impact":"An algorithm can have a rigorous error guarantee without being deterministic. The distribution and independence assumptions are part of the guarantee.","questions":["What is the simplest example of randomness as an algorithmic resource?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"Finite automata and their decision problems · Rabin and Scott","url":"https://doi.org/10.1147/rd.32.0114","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · Michael Rabin","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/rabin_9681074.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"scott","name":"Dana Scott","born":1932,"field":"logic","title":"Meaning for recursive programs","bio":"Scott contributed to automata theory, logic and the mathematical semantics of programming languages. Domain theory supplies ordered spaces in which recursive definitions have meanings.","formula":"f = F(f)","impact":"A recursive specification is a fixed-point equation. Suitable ordered structures allow a least solution to be built from increasing approximations.","questions":["What is the simplest example of meaning for recursive programs?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"Logic and programming languages · Scott","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/359810.359826","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · Dana Scott","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/scott_1193622.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"hoare","name":"C. A. R. Hoare","born":1934,"field":"systems","title":"Programs as mathematical claims","bio":"Hoare introduced a logic for program correctness and developed influential algorithms, including quicksort. His logic separates preconditions, execution and postconditions.","formula":"{P} C {Q}","impact":"A Hoare triple expresses that, when C terminates from a state satisfying P, the resulting state satisfies Q. Termination requires additional reasoning for total correctness.","questions":["What is the simplest example of programs as mathematical claims?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"ACM · C. A. R. Hoare","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/hoare_4622167.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"edmonds","name":"Jack Edmonds","born":1934,"field":"graphs","title":"Efficient combinatorial optimization","bio":"Edmonds developed polynomial-time matching algorithms and polyhedral descriptions of combinatorial problems. His work helped clarify efficient computation as a mathematical objective.","formula":"matching ↔ augmenting paths + blossoms","impact":"Odd cycles obstruct the simple bipartite augmenting-path method. Contracting a blossom preserves the essential search problem and makes a general algorithm possible.","questions":["What is the simplest example of efficient combinatorial optimization?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"INFORMS · Jack Edmonds","url":"https://www.informs.org/content/view/full/271949","type":"research"}]},{"id":"milner","name":"Robin Milner","born":1934,"field":"systems","title":"Types, proofs and communication","bio":"Milner contributed to automated reasoning, programming-language design and concurrency theory. His work includes LCF, ML and process calculi.","formula":"P ∣ Q","impact":"Concurrent processes interact rather than execute as one sequential thread. His formalisms let researchers reason systematically about communication and behavior.","questions":["What is the simplest example of types, proofs and communication?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"Elements of interaction · Milner","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/151233.151240","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · Robin Milner","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/milner_1569367.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"karp","name":"Richard Karp","born":1935,"field":"complexity","title":"A network of hard problems","bio":"Karp's 1972 paper established NP-completeness for 21 natural combinatorial problems. He also made major contributions to algorithms and randomization.","formula":"A ≤ₚ B","impact":"A polynomial-time reduction transfers an efficient algorithm for B into one for A. Starting with Cook–Levin, reductions made hardness reusable across mathematical domains.","questions":["Can you verify both directions of the clique reduction?","What must an approximation-preserving reduction preserve?","How does a randomized fingerprint avoid reading two objects repeatedly?"],"sources":[{"label":"ACM · Richard Karp and computational complexity","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/karp_3256708.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"floyd","name":"Robert Floyd","born":1936,"field":"algorithms","title":"Proving programs correct","bio":"Floyd developed foundational approaches to program verification and influential algorithms. His work ties assertions about program states to control flow.","formula":"invariant + progress → correctness","impact":"An invariant explains what remains true through a computation. A separate progress argument is needed to establish termination.","questions":["What is the simplest example of proving programs correct?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"The paradigms of programming · Floyd","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/359138.359140","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · Robert Floyd","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/floyd_3720707.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"pearl","name":"Judea Pearl","born":1936,"field":"information","title":"From association to intervention","bio":"Pearl developed influential methods for probabilistic reasoning and causal inference. Bayesian networks and structural causal models express different kinds of dependence.","formula":"Pr(Y ∣ do(X=x))","impact":"An intervention changes the mechanism generating X rather than merely conditioning on its observed value. Causal conclusions require assumptions encoded in the model.","questions":["What is the simplest example of from association to intervention?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"Causality · author’s research page","url":"https://bayes.cs.ucla.edu/","type":"research"}]},{"id":"knuth","name":"Donald Knuth","born":1938,"field":"algorithms","title":"The analysis of algorithms","bio":"Knuth systematized algorithmic knowledge in The Art of Computer Programming. He also created TeX and developed literate programming.","formula":"algorithm + proof + cost analysis","impact":"Correctness does not settle efficiency. Knuth's work combines implementation detail, combinatorial analysis and mathematical exposition.","questions":["What is the simplest example of the analysis of algorithms?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Knuth/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"cook","name":"Stephen Cook","born":1939,"field":"complexity","title":"The first universal NP problem","bio":"Cook proved that satisfiability can encode every polynomial-time nondeterministic computation. Levin independently developed closely related completeness ideas.","formula":"∀L∈NP, L ≤ₚ SAT","impact":"Cook–Levin establishes a starting point for NP-completeness. The theorem does not prove that SAT requires superpolynomial time.","questions":["What is the simplest example of the first universal np problem?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"ACM · Stephen Cook and NP-completeness","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/cook_n991950.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"hopcroft","name":"John Hopcroft","born":1939,"field":"algorithms","title":"Efficient structure in algorithms","bio":"Hopcroft contributed to automata theory and the design and analysis of algorithms. His work with Karp and Tarjan produced fundamental graph algorithms.","formula":"O(|E|√|V|)","impact":"The Hopcroft–Karp bound for bipartite matching comes from augmenting many shortest paths in phases. A structural argument controls the number of phases.","questions":["What is the simplest example of efficient structure in algorithms?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"ACM · John Hopcroft","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/hopcroft_1053917.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"szemeredi","name":"Endre Szemerédi","born":1940,"field":"graphs","title":"Order inside dense sets","bio":"Szemerédi proved that every positive-density set of integers contains arbitrarily long arithmetic progressions. He also introduced the graph regularity lemma.","formula":"a, a+d, …, a+(k−1)d","impact":"Density can force organized patterns without periodicity. His results reshaped extremal combinatorics and connected it with ergodic and additive methods.","questions":["What is the simplest example of order inside dense sets?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Szemeredi/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"deligne","name":"Pierre Deligne","born":1944,"field":"geometry","title":"The Weil conjectures","bio":"Deligne proved the remaining Riemann-hypothesis part of the Weil conjectures using the cohomological framework developed by Grothendieck and others.","formula":"|α| = q^{i/2}","impact":"Frobenius eigenvalues on the relevant cohomology have prescribed magnitudes. This links point counts over finite fields with deep geometric structure.","questions":["What is the simplest example of the weil conjectures?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Deligne/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"diffie","name":"Whitfield Diffie","born":1944,"field":"information","title":"Secrets over a public channel","bio":"Diffie and Hellman's work established public-key cryptography as a major paradigm. Key exchange allows parties to derive a shared secret using public messages.","formula":"(gᵃ)ᵇ = (gᵇ)ᵃ","impact":"The shared algebraic value is easy for participants to obtain but should be hard for an observer to recover under the chosen group's computational assumptions.","questions":["What is the simplest example of secrets over a public channel?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"New directions in cryptography · Diffie and Hellman","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1976.1055638","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"hellman","name":"Martin Hellman","born":1945,"field":"information","title":"Public-key cryptography","bio":"Hellman, with Diffie and other contributors, helped establish modern public-key cryptography. The work changed assumptions about secure communication and prior shared secrets.","formula":"g, gᵃ, gᵇ → gᵃᵇ","impact":"The algebraic identity alone is not security. Parameter choices, protocols and computational assumptions determine whether an implementation is secure.","questions":["What is the simplest example of public-key cryptography?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"New directions in cryptography · Diffie and Hellman","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1976.1055638","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · Martin Hellman","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/hellman_4055781.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"adleman","name":"Leonard Adleman","born":1945,"field":"information","title":"Computation across representations","bio":"Adleman co-developed RSA with Rivest and Shamir. He later demonstrated a molecular computation using DNA to solve a small combinatorial instance.","formula":"c = mᵉ mod N","impact":"RSA uses a trapdoor-style arithmetic structure; secure encryption additionally requires an appropriate encoding protocol. Adleman's DNA work explored a very different computational substrate.","questions":["What is the simplest example of computation across representations?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"ACM · Leonard Adleman","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/adleman_7308544.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"yao","name":"Andrew Yao","born":1946,"field":"complexity","title":"Randomness, communication and security","bio":"Yao developed fundamental ideas in randomized algorithms, communication complexity and cryptography. His minimax principle is a central lower-bound tool.","formula":"randomized worst case ↔ distributional deterministic analysis","impact":"Under the relevant finite minimax formulation, a hard input distribution can lower-bound randomized algorithms. This changes a randomized lower bound into a distributional problem.","questions":["What is the simplest example of randomness, communication and security?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"ACM · Andrew Yao","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/yao_1611524.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"thurston","name":"William Thurston","born":1946,"field":"geometry","title":"Three-dimensional geometry","bio":"Thurston transformed low-dimensional topology through hyperbolic geometry and the geometrization program. He showed how geometry can organize the classification of three-manifolds.","formula":"topological decomposition → geometric pieces","impact":"A complicated manifold can be studied through canonical pieces carrying geometric structures. Perelman's later work completed geometrization via Ricci flow.","questions":["What is the simplest example of three-dimensional geometry?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Thurston/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"connes","name":"Alain Connes","born":1947,"field":"geometry","title":"Geometry without commuting coordinates","bio":"Connes developed noncommutative geometry and major parts of operator-algebra theory. Algebraic structures become geometric objects even when multiplication is noncommutative.","formula":"ab ≠ ba","impact":"Replacing functions on a space by a noncommutative algebra extends geometric reasoning to new settings. The connection also reaches mathematical physics and quantum theory.","questions":["What is the simplest example of geometry without commuting coordinates?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Connes/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"rivest","name":"Ron Rivest","born":1947,"field":"information","title":"Public-key algorithms","bio":"Rivest co-developed RSA and contributed broadly to algorithms and cryptography. His work connects computational difficulty with practical constructions.","formula":"m ↦ mᵉ mod N","impact":"A public transformation can be easy to apply while reversal requires secret information or a hard computation. Bare algebraic RSA is not a complete secure encryption protocol.","questions":["What is the simplest example of public-key algorithms?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"ACM · Ron Rivest","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/rivest_1403005.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"levin","name":"Leonid Levin","born":1948,"field":"complexity","title":"Universal search and completeness","bio":"Levin independently established foundational completeness results and developed algorithmic information theory and average-case complexity.","formula":"search + verification + resource bounds","impact":"Worst-case difficulty and typical-instance difficulty are different. Levin's frameworks make the input distribution and verification process part of the mathematical problem.","questions":["What is the simplest example of universal search and completeness?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"Leonid Levin · selected research and exposition","url":"https://www.cs.bu.edu/~lnd/","type":"research"}]},{"id":"tarjan","name":"Robert Tarjan","born":1948,"field":"algorithms","title":"Data structures with provable efficiency","bio":"Tarjan developed fundamental graph algorithms and data-structure analyses, often with collaborators. Amortized analysis measures the cost of a sequence rather than one isolated operation.","formula":"total cost / number of operations","impact":"A single expensive step need not make a data structure inefficient. Structural accounting can show that expensive operations cannot occur too often.","questions":["What is the simplest example of data structures with provable efficiency?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"ACM · Robert Tarjan","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/tarjan_1092048.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"lovasz","name":"László Lovász","born":1948,"field":"graphs","title":"Discrete questions, richer spaces","bio":"Lovász connected combinatorics with probability, topology, optimization and graph limits. His local lemma, theta function and topological methods became major tools.","formula":"α(G) ≤ ϑ(G) ≤ χ(Ḡ)","impact":"The theta function bridges an intractable combinatorial quantity and semidefinite optimization. It also gives a multiplicative upper bound for Shannon capacity.","questions":["Why is pairwise independence not enough for the Local Lemma?","Can you derive the theta SDP from an independent set?","What information does a graphon retain that a local graph limit does not?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lovasz/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"valiant","name":"Leslie Valiant","born":1949,"field":"complexity","title":"Counting, circuits and learning","bio":"Valiant introduced counting complexity, developed algebraic complexity and formulated PAC learning. He showed that changing the information requested can change a problem's complexity.","formula":"perm(A) = Σσ ∏ᵢ aᵢ,σ(i)","impact":"For a zero–one bipartite adjacency matrix, the permanent counts perfect matchings. Computing it is #P-complete under polynomial-time counting reductions.","questions":["Why do counting reductions need more than decision reductions?","What changes in the permanent over characteristic two?","Can a class need few examples but still be hard to learn?"],"sources":[{"label":"ACM · Leslie Valiant's research","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/valiant_2612174.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"papadimitriou","name":"Christos Papadimitriou","born":1949,"field":"complexity","title":"The complexity of guaranteed solutions","bio":"Papadimitriou contributed to complexity, databases, optimization and algorithmic game theory. His PPAD class formalizes total search based on directed parity.","formula":"PPAD ⊆ TFNP","impact":"A succinct directed graph with a known path endpoint must have another. Existence is guaranteed, but following the path may take exponentially many steps.","questions":["What is the simplest example of the complexity of guaranteed solutions?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"Christos Papadimitriou · Columbia Engineering","url":"https://www.engineering.columbia.edu/faculty-staff/directory/christos-papadimitriou","type":"research"}]},{"id":"khachiyan","name":"Leonid Khachiyan","born":1952,"field":"algorithms","title":"Linear programming in polynomial time","bio":"Khachiyan established a polynomial-time algorithm for linear programming using the ellipsoid method. The result was a major theoretical advance in optimization.","formula":"LP ∈ P","impact":"Linear optimization's complexity could now be bounded polynomially in the input bit length. This was a worst-case theoretical result, not a claim that ellipsoid is always the fastest method in practice.","questions":["What is the simplest example of linear programming in polynomial time?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"INFORMS · Leonid Khachiyan and linear programming","url":"https://pubsonline.informs.org/do/10.1287/orms.2005.03.27in/full","type":"research"}]},{"id":"shamir","name":"Adi Shamir","born":1952,"field":"complexity","title":"Interaction reaches polynomial space","bio":"Shamir proved IP=PSPACE, extending the algebraic interactive-proof approach of Lund, Fortnow, Karloff and Nisan. He also co-developed RSA and introduced secret sharing.","formula":"IP = PSPACE","impact":"A polynomial-time verifier can check a polynomial-space computation through randomized interaction with an unlimited prover. The theorem concerns verification, not efficient computation of the answer alone.","questions":["Can you derive every quantifier operator on {0,1}?","Where exactly is degree reduction checked in the protocol?","How does a false claim survive successive random challenges?"],"sources":[{"label":"IP = PSPACE · Adi Shamir","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/146585.146609","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"wiles","name":"Andrew Wiles","born":1953,"field":"algebra","title":"An arithmetic problem through modularity","bio":"Wiles proved a major modularity result for semistable elliptic curves, with a key repair completed jointly with Taylor. It implied Fermat's Last Theorem.","formula":"xⁿ + yⁿ ≠ zⁿ for positive integers, n>2","impact":"The proof links Diophantine equations to elliptic curves and modular forms. A centuries-old numerical statement was resolved by a much larger structural theory.","questions":["What is the simplest example of an arithmetic problem through modularity?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Wiles/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"micali","name":"Silvio Micali","born":1954,"field":"information","title":"Security with mathematical definitions","bio":"Micali helped establish modern cryptography through probabilistic encryption, interactive proofs and zero knowledge, working with Goldwasser and other collaborators.","formula":"real view ≈ simulated view","impact":"Simulation defines what a verifier can learn without listing every possible secret fact. Computational indistinguishability formalizes the inability of efficient observers to tell distributions apart.","questions":["What is the simplest example of security with mathematical definitions?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"The Knowledge Complexity of Interactive Proof Systems · GMR","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/0218012","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · Silvio Micali","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/micali_9954407.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"karmarkar","name":"Narendra Karmarkar","born":1955,"field":"algorithms","title":"An interior route through optimization","bio":"Karmarkar introduced a polynomial-time interior-point approach to linear programming. It helped transform both the theory and practice of optimization.","formula":"interior iterates → optimum","impact":"Rather than move along the edges of a feasible polytope, an interior-point method navigates its interior. Careful geometry and numerical analysis control progress.","questions":["What is the simplest example of an interior route through optimization?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"A new polynomial-time algorithm for linear programming · Karmarkar","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02579150","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"goldwasser","name":"Shafi Goldwasser","born":1958,"field":"information","title":"Proof without disclosure","bio":"Goldwasser co-developed probabilistic encryption and, with Micali and Rackoff, interactive proofs and zero knowledge. Her work made security a subject of precise computational definitions.","formula":"View(P↔V*) ≈ S(x)","impact":"A simulator without the secret witness must reproduce the verifier's view in the specified sense. Security against arbitrary verifiers is stronger than merely simulating an honest transcript.","questions":["What changes between honest-verifier and general zero knowledge?","Why can simulation use rewinding when a real prover cannot?","How do commitments extend zero knowledge to all of NP?"],"sources":[{"label":"The Knowledge Complexity of Interactive Proof Systems · GMR","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/0218012","type":"paper"},{"label":"ACM · Shafi Goldwasser","url":"https://amturing.acm.org/award_winners/goldwasser_8627889.cfm","type":"research"}]},{"id":"gowers","name":"Timothy Gowers","born":1963,"field":"graphs","title":"Uniformity measures structure","bio":"Gowers developed methods in Banach-space theory and additive combinatorics. His uniformity norms organize higher-order patterns and supplied a new proof of Szemerédi's theorem.","formula":"∥f∥Uᵏ","impact":"Uniformity norms detect structured correlations beyond ordinary Fourier coefficients. They connect additive patterns with higher-order analytic structure.","questions":["What is the simplest example of uniformity measures structure?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Gowers/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"perelman","name":"Grigori Perelman","born":1966,"field":"geometry","title":"Topology through geometric flow","bio":"Perelman completed decisive parts of Hamilton's Ricci-flow program, proving geometrization and the Poincaré conjecture. His work controlled singularities in a changing geometry.","formula":"∂g/∂t = −2 Ric(g)","impact":"Ricci flow evolves a metric according to curvature. Understanding its singularities and surgery lets one extract topological information.","questions":["What is the simplest example of topology through geometric flow?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Perelman/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"tao","name":"Terence Tao","born":1975,"field":"analysis","title":"Methods across mathematical borders","bio":"Tao has made major contributions to harmonic analysis, partial differential equations, number theory and combinatorics. With Green he proved that the primes contain arbitrarily long arithmetic progressions.","formula":"p, p+d, …, p+(k−1)d","impact":"The primes have zero density among integers, so a direct application of Szemerédi's theorem is insufficient. Transference and pseudorandomness bridge that gap.","questions":["What is the simplest example of methods across mathematical borders?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Tao/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"mirzakhani","name":"Maryam Mirzakhani","born":1977,"field":"geometry","title":"The geometry of all surfaces","bio":"Mirzakhani made major advances in hyperbolic geometry, moduli spaces and dynamics. She connected the counting of curves on surfaces with volumes of parameter spaces.","formula":"N(L) ∼ c L^{6g−6}","impact":"For a fixed closed hyperbolic surface of genus g≥2, suitable counts of simple closed geodesics have polynomial asymptotics. The constant depends on the surface and counting convention.","questions":["What is the simplest example of the geometry of all surfaces?","Which assumptions are essential to the signature result?","Which connected idea would you explore next?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Mirzakhani/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"babai","name":"László Babai","born":1950,"field":"complexity","title":"Symmetry becomes an algorithm","bio":"Babai developed influential graph-isomorphism and permutation-group algorithms and helped establish Arthur–Merlin protocols. His quasipolynomial isomorphism algorithm combines combinatorial and algebraic structure.","formula":"GI ∈ TIME(exp((log n)^O(1)))","impact":"A large symmetry group can be represented by generators and studied through its actions. Large automorphism groups alone do not make an isomorphism instance hard.","questions":["Why do equally sized regular graphs defeat 1-WL?","How can a generating set encode a huge permutation group?","How does GI∈coAM constrain an NP-completeness claim?"],"sources":[{"label":"Graph Isomorphism in Quasipolynomial Time","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03547","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"razborov","name":"Alexander Razborov","born":1963,"field":"complexity","title":"Lower bounds and their barriers","bio":"Razborov developed major circuit lower-bound methods. With Rudich he formulated the natural-proofs barrier, relating certain lower-bound strategies to pseudorandomness.","formula":"small circuit → simple approximation","impact":"If a restricted circuit admits a low-complexity approximation that the target function cannot admit, the circuit must be large. Restrictions on the circuit model are essential.","questions":["How does the monotone approximation method work?","Why does field characteristic matter for modular gates?","What is the input length in the natural-proofs constructivity condition?"],"sources":[{"label":"Natural Proofs · Razborov and Rudich","url":"https://doi.org/10.1006/jcss.1997.1494","type":"paper"},{"label":"Natural Proofs · original paper copy","url":"https://www1.karlin.mff.cuni.cz/~krajicek/rr.pdf","type":"research"}]},{"id":"nisan","name":"Noam Nisan","born":1961,"field":"complexity","title":"Randomness for limited memory","bio":"Nisan constructed pseudorandom generators for space-bounded computation and developed hardness-versus-randomness methods with Wigderson. He also contributed to communication complexity and algorithmic economics.","formula":"G : {0,1}ˢ → {0,1}ᴿ","impact":"A distribution can fool a resource-bounded observer without being truly uniform. The observer model and error parameter determine the required seed length.","questions":["How is space-bounded computation represented by a branching program?","Why does a hybrid argument expose one predictable output bit?","Which hardness assumption is strong enough for logarithmic seeds?"],"sources":[{"label":"Nisan–Wigderson: Hardness vs Randomness","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-0000(05)80043-1","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"wigderson","name":"Avi Wigderson","born":1956,"field":"complexity","title":"Hardness can replace randomness","bio":"Wigderson's work connects cryptography, pseudorandomness, lower bounds and optimization. With Nisan and Impagliazzo he developed major hardness-versus-randomness results.","formula":"strong circuit hardness ⇒ P = BPP","impact":"The hypothesis is a sufficiently strong circuit lower bound for an efficiently describable function, not merely P≠NP. Enumerating logarithmic-length seeds then gives deterministic simulation.","questions":["How does the hybrid argument produce a predictor?","What is the distinction between worst-case and average-case hardness?","Why can derandomization itself imply circuit lower bounds?"],"sources":[{"label":"Mathematics and Computation · author’s book page","url":"https://www.math.ias.edu/avi/book","type":"research"}]},{"id":"lipton","name":"Richard Lipton","born":1946,"field":"complexity","title":"Small circuits, large consequences","bio":"Lipton contributed to complexity, verification and randomized algebraic algorithms. The Karp–Lipton theorem connects small circuits for NP to a collapse of the polynomial hierarchy.","formula":"NP ⊆ P/poly ⇒ PH = Σ₂ᴾ","impact":"A hypothetical small SAT circuit can be used to produce witnesses. Existentially guessing the circuit and universally checking its outputs compresses quantifier alternation.","questions":["Can you write the quantifier collapse without a correctness oracle?","Why does SAT self-reducibility turn decision into search?","How do randomized polynomial evaluations certify inequality?"],"sources":[{"label":"Karp–Lipton · Some connections between nonuniform and uniform complexity classes","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/800141.804678","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"polya","name":"George Pólya","born":1887,"field":"analysis","title":"Probability, symmetry and discovery","bio":"Pólya contributed to random walks, enumeration, analysis and mathematical problem solving. His recurrence theorem shows a sharp dimensional transition for lattice random walks.","formula":"Z¹, Z² recurrent; Zᵈ transient for d≥3","impact":"Return probabilities decay at a rate whose sum diverges in dimensions one and two. His enumeration theorem separately turns symmetry counting into cycle-index algebra.","questions":["Why does divergence of return probabilities characterize recurrence?","How would reflections change the square-coloring calculation?","Can a mathematical heuristic be expressed as an algorithm?"],"sources":[{"label":"MacTutor · historical biography","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Polya/","type":"biography"}]},{"id":"fortnow","name":"Lance Fortnow","born":1963,"field":"complexity","title":"The power of separated provers","bio":"Fortnow, with Babai and Lund, proved MIP=NEXP. He also contributed to the algebraic interactive-proof methods behind the 1990 advances in verification.","formula":"MIP = NEXP","impact":"A polynomial-time verifier can cross-check separated provers about an exponentially large claimed computation. Noncommunication constrains coordinated cheating strategies.","questions":["Why do provers who agree beforehand still face consistency constraints?","How does a low-degree encoding represent an exponential object?","How does a verifier’s acceptance probability become a CSP optimum?"],"sources":[{"label":"Non-Deterministic Exponential Time Has Two-Prover Interactive Protocols","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01200056","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"vidick","name":"Thomas Vidick","born":1982,"field":"complexity","title":"Quantum correlations and verification","bio":"Vidick works on quantum information and complexity. With Ji, Natarajan, Wright and Yuen, he proved MIP*=RE, connecting entangled provers with undecidability.","formula":"MIP* = RE","impact":"The verifier is classical, while the provers may share entanglement of unbounded finite dimension. The equality does not give an ordinary algorithm for deciding the halting problem.","questions":["How can entanglement correlate answers without communication?","Why does unbounded finite dimension matter?","What distinguishes a verifier from an algorithm deciding the language?"],"sources":[{"label":"MIP* = RE · Ji, Natarajan, Vidick, Wright and Yuen","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.04383","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"hastad","name":"Johan Håstad","born":1960,"field":"complexity","title":"The sharp edge of approximation","bio":"Håstad proved landmark circuit lower bounds and optimal inapproximability results. His PCP constructions exposed quantitative limits on efficient approximation.","formula":"MAX-E3SAT: threshold 7/8","impact":"For clauses with three distinct variables, a random assignment satisfies each with probability 7/8. Beating the corresponding approximation threshold is NP-hard, up to any fixed positive slack.","questions":["How is each verifier random string turned into a constraint?","Why is a tiny exact-satisfaction gap useless for constant-factor hardness?","How does Fourier information recover a label from a long code?"],"sources":[{"label":"Some Optimal Inapproximability Results · author’s copy","url":"https://www.csc.kth.se/~johanh/optimalinap.pdf","type":"research"}]},{"id":"khot","name":"Subhash Khot","born":1978,"field":"complexity","title":"A conjecture that reorganized a field","bio":"Khot introduced the Unique Games Conjecture. Its consequences link approximation thresholds with Fourier analysis, Gaussian geometry and semidefinite programming.","formula":"xᵥ = πᵤᵥ(xᵤ)","impact":"A permutation constraint determines one label from the other. The conjecture concerns distinguishing nearly satisfiable systems from systems of very low optimum, not exact satisfiability.","questions":["Why is exact satisfiability easier than the promised approximation gap?","What role does the alphabet size play?","How does a dictatorship test encode the same geometry as SDP rounding?"],"sources":[{"label":"On the power of unique 2-prover 1-round games","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/509907.510017","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"odonnell","name":"Ryan O’Donnell","born":1979,"field":"analysis","title":"Fourier analysis on the Boolean cube","bio":"O'Donnell developed and systematized analytic methods for Boolean functions. With Mossel and Oleszkiewicz he proved an invariance principle and the Majority Is Stablest theorem.","formula":"f(x) = ΣS f̂(S) ∏ᵢ∈S xᵢ","impact":"Fourier coefficients encode how a function depends on its coordinates. Noise stability and influence connect these coefficients to learning, PCPs and approximation.","questions":["Can you derive the Fourier formula for noise stability?","Why must balance and the range of ρ be specified?","How can an influential coordinate be decoded as a CSP label?"],"sources":[{"label":"Analysis of Boolean Functions · author’s book","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.10386","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"goemans","name":"Michel Goemans","born":1964,"field":"algorithms","title":"Round geometry into a cut","bio":"Goemans and Williamson developed the celebrated semidefinite-programming approximation algorithm for MAX-CUT. It turns a vector solution into a cut by a random hyperplane.","formula":"E[cut] ≥ 0.87856 · OPT","impact":"The separation probability of vectors at angle θ is θ/π. Comparing this with their SDP contribution yields the approximation guarantee.","questions":["Can you prove the θ/π separation probability geometrically?","Why is the SDP optimum an upper bound on MAX-CUT?","Can a small graph exhibit a strict integrality gap?"],"sources":[{"label":"Improved approximation algorithms for maximum cut and satisfiability problems","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/227683.227684","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"raghavendra","name":"Prasad Raghavendra","born":1981,"field":"algorithms","title":"A unified theory of CSP approximation","bio":"Raghavendra proved that a canonical SDP framework gives optimal approximation guarantees for finite constraint languages, conditional on the Unique Games Conjecture.","formula":"UGC ⇒ optimality of the basic SDP framework","impact":"The theorem concerns fixed finite-domain, bounded-arity maximum CSPs and asymptotic approximation guarantees. It is not a claim that SDP solves every optimization problem exactly.","questions":["Why do higher-arity predicates need local distributions?","What is the difference between an integrality gap and NP-hardness?","How does the rounding guarantee become a dictatorship test?"],"sources":[{"label":"Optimal algorithms and inapproximability results for every CSP?","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1374376.1374414","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"lasserre","name":"Jean-Bernard Lasserre","born":1953,"field":"algorithms","title":"Optimization through moments","bio":"Lasserre developed a hierarchy of semidefinite relaxations for polynomial optimization. Moments and sums of squares connect algorithms with algebraic certificates.","formula":"Ẽ[p²] ≥ 0","impact":"A low-degree pseudoexpectation satisfies selected consistency and positivity conditions without necessarily coming from a real distribution. Higher levels impose stronger conditions at greater cost.","questions":["Can you build the degree-two moment matrix for three variables?","How does a pseudoexpectation certify a proof-system lower bound?","Which additional moment constraints first detect an inconsistent cycle?"],"sources":[{"label":"Global Optimization with Polynomials and the Problem of Moments","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/S1052623400366802","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"weisfeiler","name":"Boris Weisfeiler","born":1941,"field":"graphs","title":"Refining the information in a graph","bio":"Weisfeiler worked in algebra and group theory. With Andrei Leman, he developed graph-refinement ideas that became central to isomorphism, finite model theory and later machine-learning expressiveness.","formula":"cₜ₊₁(v) = encode(cₜ(v), multiset{cₜ(u):u∼v})","impact":"Vertex color refinement is an isomorphism invariant, not a complete test. Equally sized regular graphs already show that local aggregation can miss global differences.","questions":["Can you exhibit two connected regular graphs that 1-WL cannot distinguish?","What global parity does a CFI gadget encode?","How does a pebble game represent bounded-variable logic?"],"sources":[{"label":"A Lower Bound on the Number of Variables in First Order Logic","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01305232","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"immerman","name":"Neil Immerman","born":1954,"field":"logic","title":"Computation described by logic","bio":"Immerman helped establish descriptive complexity and independently proved closure of nondeterministic space under complement. His work connects machines, logical languages and graph algorithms.","formula":"NL = coNL","impact":"Inductive counting can certify non-reachability in logarithmic space. On ordered finite structures, fixed-point logic also captures polynomial-time computation.","questions":["How can an exact count certify that no reachable vertex was omitted?","Where does the order enter the characterization of P?","How does WL correspond to counting logic rather than ordinary FO?"],"sources":[{"label":"Languages that Capture Complexity Classes","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/0216051","type":"paper"},{"label":"Nondeterministic Space is Closed Under Complementation","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/0217058","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"gaifman","name":"Haim Gaifman","born":1934,"field":"logic","title":"The locality of first-order logic","bio":"Gaifman proved a normal-form theorem expressing first-order sentences through local properties and separated witnesses. It became foundational in finite model theory.","formula":"local types + distant witnesses","impact":"Bounded logical depth limits the scale of local inspection. It does not mean that first-order truth is determined solely by frequencies seen from a random vertex.","questions":["Can one exceptional vertex change FO truth without changing a local limit?","What do separated witnesses add to purely local formulas?","Why does a sparse neighborhood need not have bounded size?"],"sources":[{"label":"On Local and Non-Local Properties · original chapter","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0049-237X(08)71879-2","type":"paper"}]},{"id":"nesetril","name":"Jaroslav Nešetřil","born":1946,"field":"graphs","title":"Sparsity with structural meaning","bio":"Nešetřil shaped graph homomorphism theory, Ramsey theory and structural sparsity. With Ossona de Mendez he developed bounded-expansion and nowhere-dense classes.","formula":"∀r ∃f(r): |E(H)|/|V(H)| ≤ f(r)","impact":"For bounded expansion, this bound applies to every depth-r shallow minor H. Sparse structure remains sparse after bounded-radius contractions.","questions":["Can you construct the shallow minor that recovers a subdivided clique?","How do low tree-depth colorings help evaluate an FO sentence?","How do homomorphism constraints relate to local graph specifications?"],"sources":[{"label":"Characterisations and Examples of Graph Classes with Bounded Expansion","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.3265","type":"paper"},{"label":"Sparsity: Graphs, Structures, and Algorithms","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-27875-4","type":"paper"}]}],"fields":{"geometry":{"name":"Geometry & topology","color":"#766680","soft":"#eee8ef","symbol":"∂"},"analysis":{"name":"Analysis & probability","color":"#426c79","soft":"#e4eef0","symbol":"∫"},"algebra":{"name":"Algebra & number theory","color":"#a17836","soft":"#f1eadc","symbol":"ℤ"},"logic":{"name":"Logic & foundations","color":"#696c48","soft":"#ececde","symbol":"∀"},"systems":{"name":"Programming & systems","color":"#7f6757","soft":"#eee7df","symbol":"⌘"},"information":{"name":"Information & cryptography","color":"#47736a","soft":"#e2ece7","symbol":"⊕"},"complexity":{"name":"Computation & complexity","color":"#b44d35","soft":"#f3e5dc","symbol":"λ"},"graphs":{"name":"Combinatorics & graphs","color":"#3e6d58","soft":"#e1ebe2","symbol":"χ"},"algorithms":{"name":"Algorithms & optimization","color":"#536184","soft":"#e7eaf1","symbol":"≲"}},"connections":[{"source":"euclid","target":"hilbert","label":"Axiomatization","description":"Hilbert revisited the axiomatic foundations of geometry."},{"source":"euclid","target":"descartes","label":"Coordinates","description":"A conceptual bridge from synthetic geometry to algebraic geometry in coordinates."},{"source":"archimedes","target":"newton","label":"Areas and limits","description":"A reading connection from exhaustion arguments to integral calculus."},{"source":"archimedes","target":"leibniz","label":"Areas and limits","description":"A conceptual predecessor-to-calculus route, not a claim of direct mentorship."},{"source":"diophantus","target":"fermat","label":"Diophantine equations","description":"Fermat's work grew in conversation with the arithmetic tradition represented by Arithmetica."},{"source":"aryabhata","target":"al-khwarizmi","label":"Algorithmic arithmetic","description":"A thematic comparison of computational mathematics across traditions."},{"source":"al-khwarizmi","target":"fibonacci","label":"Transmission of arithmetic","description":"A reading route through the spread of Hindu–Arabic calculation."},{"source":"khayyam","target":"descartes","label":"Equations and curves","description":"Compare conic solutions of cubics with coordinate geometry."},{"source":"fibonacci","target":"knuth","label":"Recurrences","description":"Fibonacci recurrences recur in combinatorial and algorithmic analysis."},{"source":"descartes","target":"newton","label":"Coordinates and change","description":"Coordinate geometry provides a language for calculus and mechanics."},{"source":"fermat","target":"pascal","label":"Probability","description":"Their correspondence developed reasoning about games of chance."},{"source":"fermat","target":"gauss","label":"Modular arithmetic","description":"Arithmetic congruences connect the two bodies of work."},{"source":"fermat","target":"wiles","label":"Fermat's Last Theorem","description":"Wiles's modularity work supplied the final route to the theorem."},{"source":"pascal","target":"laplace","label":"Probability","description":"A reading route from elementary chances to a systematic probabilistic theory."},{"source":"newton","target":"euler","label":"Calculus and mechanics","description":"Euler developed analysis and mechanics in the calculus tradition."},{"source":"leibniz","target":"euler","label":"Calculus notation","description":"The differential and integral calculus became central to Euler's analysis."},{"source":"leibniz","target":"boole","label":"Symbolic reasoning","description":"A conceptual route from symbolic calculation to algebraic logic."},{"source":"euler","target":"lagrange","label":"Analysis and mechanics","description":"Lagrange further developed analytical methods in mechanics and variation."},{"source":"euler","target":"fourier","label":"Series","description":"A mathematical bridge through expansions and analytic representations."},{"source":"euler","target":"tutte","label":"Graph structure","description":"The bridge problem opens a reading route into structural graph theory."},{"source":"lagrange","target":"dantzig","label":"Constrained optimization","description":"Compare nonlinear multiplier conditions with linear optimization."},{"source":"laplace","target":"markov","label":"Probability","description":"A thematic route from probabilistic analysis to dependent random processes."},{"source":"fourier","target":"odonnell","label":"Fourier analysis","description":"Boolean-cube Fourier analysis is a finite harmonic-analysis counterpart."},{"source":"gauss","target":"riemann","label":"Geometry","description":"Riemann's intrinsic geometry develops the study of curvature beyond surfaces."},{"source":"cauchy","target":"lebesgue","label":"Analysis and integration","description":"A route through increasingly flexible foundations of analysis."},{"source":"galois","target":"cayley","label":"Groups","description":"Permutation symmetries of equations connect to abstract group structure."},{"source":"galois","target":"noether","label":"Structural algebra","description":"A reading bridge from Galois groups to modern algebraic methods."},{"source":"boole","target":"shannon","label":"Switching circuits","description":"Shannon applied Boolean algebra to electrical switching circuits."},{"source":"lovelace","target":"hopper","label":"Programming","description":"A historical reading bridge between symbolic programs and automatic programming."},{"source":"cayley","target":"babai","label":"Permutation groups","description":"Groups acting on finite sets become algorithmic data structures."},{"source":"riemann","target":"poincare","label":"Manifolds and topology","description":"The geometry of spaces leads to global topological invariants."},{"source":"riemann","target":"atiyah","label":"Geometry and analysis","description":"Differential-geometric structure enters the index theorem."},{"source":"dedekind","target":"noether","label":"Ideals","description":"The structural study of ideals is central to modern algebra."},{"source":"cantor","target":"godel","label":"Infinity and foundations","description":"Set theory and diagonal methods connect these foundational programs."},{"source":"cantor","target":"turing","label":"Diagonalization","description":"A conceptual link between uncountability and noncomputability arguments."},{"source":"frege","target":"russell","label":"Foundations","description":"Russell's paradox challenged unrestricted foundations including Frege's system."},{"source":"russell","target":"godel","label":"Formal arithmetic","description":"The Principia Mathematica tradition forms part of the setting for incompleteness."},{"source":"poincare","target":"thurston","label":"Topology","description":"A route from foundational topology to geometric three-manifold theory."},{"source":"markov","target":"polya","label":"Random walks","description":"Lattice walks are a central class of Markov chains."},{"source":"markov","target":"kolmogorov","label":"Stochastic processes","description":"Transition-based probability sits inside the modern axiomatic framework."},{"source":"peano","target":"godel","label":"Formal arithmetic","description":"An effective arithmetic theory is the setting in which incompleteness operates."},{"source":"hilbert","target":"godel","label":"Limits of formal proof","description":"Incompleteness constrained important ambitions of Hilbert's foundational program."},{"source":"hilbert","target":"noether","label":"Algebra","description":"Finite generation and structural algebra connect their mathematical work."},{"source":"hilbert","target":"lasserre","label":"Algebraic certificates","description":"A conceptual route from polynomial impossibility certificates to positivity certificates."},{"source":"lebesgue","target":"kolmogorov","label":"Measure and probability","description":"Measure theory provides the language for modern probability axioms."},{"source":"lebesgue","target":"banach","label":"Function spaces","description":"Integration and normed function spaces meet in functional analysis."},{"source":"hardy","target":"ramanujan","label":"Collaboration","description":"Their work includes the partition asymptotic and analytic number theory."},{"source":"brouwer","target":"nash","label":"Fixed points","description":"Nash's equilibrium existence proof uses a fixed-point theorem."},{"source":"brouwer","target":"papadimitriou","label":"Computational fixed points","description":"Finding a guaranteed fixed point is a central PPAD-type question."},{"source":"noether","target":"grothendieck","label":"Structural algebra","description":"Rings, ideals and modules supply part of the algebraic language of schemes."},{"source":"ramanujan","target":"tao","label":"Arithmetic patterns","description":"A thematic reading link across analytic and additive number theory."},{"source":"banach","target":"von-neumann","label":"Functional analysis","description":"Operators on infinite-dimensional spaces connect their work."},{"source":"wiener","target":"shannon","label":"Noise and communication","description":"A thematic bridge between stochastic analysis, control and information."},{"source":"post","target":"turing","label":"Computability","description":"Distinct formal models and undecidable problems illuminate the same boundary."},{"source":"tarski","target":"gaifman","label":"Model theory","description":"Semantic structure and logical expressiveness lead toward locality."},{"source":"kolmogorov","target":"levin","label":"Algorithmic information","description":"Levin developed fundamental parts of the algorithmic-information program."},{"source":"kolmogorov","target":"erdos","label":"Probability and combinatorics","description":"The probabilistic method uses probability as a tool for deterministic existence."},{"source":"church","target":"turing","label":"Models of computation","description":"Lambda calculus and Turing machines define equivalent computability notions."},{"source":"church","target":"kleene","label":"Recursion theory","description":"Kleene developed the lambda-calculus and recursive-function tradition."},{"source":"church","target":"mccarthy","label":"Functional computation","description":"Lambda notation and function application influenced Lisp's language of computation."},{"source":"von-neumann","target":"nash","label":"Game theory","description":"Nash extends equilibrium existence beyond the zero-sum setting."},{"source":"von-neumann","target":"connes","label":"Operator algebras","description":"Noncommutative geometry develops an operator-algebraic tradition."},{"source":"von-neumann","target":"ulam","label":"Monte Carlo","description":"They collaborated in the development of Monte Carlo methods."},{"source":"hopper","target":"backus","label":"High-level languages","description":"A reading bridge from automatic programming to FORTRAN and compiler design."},{"source":"whitney","target":"edmonds","label":"Matroids","description":"Independence axioms become algorithmic and polyhedral tools."},{"source":"whitney","target":"tutte","label":"Graph structure","description":"Matroids and structural graph theory connect their work."},{"source":"kleene","target":"scott","label":"Logic and semantics","description":"Formal computation and fixed points lead into denotational semantics."},{"source":"ulam","target":"erdos","label":"Combinatorics","description":"A thematic link through combinatorial questions and probabilistic reasoning."},{"source":"turing","target":"cook","label":"Computation to complexity","description":"Cook–Levin measures the cost of computations in a formal machine model."},{"source":"turing","target":"shannon","label":"Information processing","description":"A conceptual comparison between computability and communication limits."},{"source":"erdos","target":"lovasz","label":"Local Lemma","description":"The Erdős–Lovász Local Lemma strengthens probabilistic existence arguments."},{"source":"erdos","target":"szemeredi","label":"Density and structure","description":"Extremal and additive combinatorics connect their mathematical programs."},{"source":"dantzig","target":"khachiyan","label":"Linear programming","description":"Khachiyan established a polynomial-time worst-case algorithm for LP."},{"source":"dantzig","target":"karmarkar","label":"Interior-point methods","description":"Karmarkar supplied another polynomial-time route through linear optimization."},{"source":"shannon","target":"lovasz","label":"Zero-error capacity","description":"Lovász's theta function solved Shannon's capacity problem for C₅."},{"source":"shannon","target":"diffie","label":"Information and security","description":"A conceptual route from information theory to computational cryptography."},{"source":"simon","target":"newell","label":"Heuristic problem solving","description":"They collaborated on early reasoning programs and cognitive models."},{"source":"tutte","target":"edmonds","label":"Perfect matchings","description":"Structural characterizations and algorithms meet in matching theory."},{"source":"bellman","target":"dijkstra","label":"Optimal substructure","description":"Dynamic programming and greedy shortest paths expose different optimality arguments."},{"source":"codd","target":"immerman","label":"Queries and logic","description":"Relational query languages connect databases with descriptive complexity."},{"source":"backus","target":"milner","label":"Programming languages","description":"Language design connects through types and functional programming."},{"source":"serre","target":"grothendieck","label":"Algebraic geometry","description":"Their work and correspondence are central to the development of the subject."},{"source":"grothendieck","target":"deligne","label":"Étale cohomology","description":"Deligne proved the final Weil conjecture in the cohomological framework."},{"source":"grothendieck","target":"valiant","label":"Geometry and complexity","description":"A thematic bridge, not direct mentorship: polynomial complexity invites geometric methods."},{"source":"atiyah","target":"connes","label":"Noncommutative geometry","description":"Index theory and operator algebras meet in noncommutative geometry."},{"source":"mccarthy","target":"minsky","label":"Artificial intelligence","description":"Both helped shape the early research field of artificial intelligence."},{"source":"minsky","target":"newell","label":"Models of intelligence","description":"Compare representation-centered and problem-solving-centered approaches."},{"source":"newell","target":"polya","label":"Proof search","description":"A conceptual reading connection between mathematical heuristics and search programs."},{"source":"nash","target":"papadimitriou","label":"Equilibrium computation","description":"Equilibrium existence becomes a total-search complexity problem."},{"source":"dijkstra","target":"hoare","label":"Program correctness","description":"Invariants and logical assertions turn programs into objects of proof."},{"source":"dijkstra","target":"tarjan","label":"Graph algorithms","description":"A reading link through provably efficient graph computations."},{"source":"rabin","target":"karp","label":"String fingerprints","description":"Rabin–Karp uses compact hashes for string matching."},{"source":"rabin","target":"yao","label":"Randomized complexity","description":"Yao's minimax perspective provides tools for understanding randomized algorithms."},{"source":"scott","target":"milner","label":"Semantics and languages","description":"Domain-theoretic semantics and practical typed languages connect."},{"source":"hoare","target":"floyd","label":"Assertions and invariants","description":"Their work helped establish formal methods for program correctness."},{"source":"edmonds","target":"lovasz","label":"Combinatorial optimization","description":"Matching, matroids and convex optimization link their contributions."},{"source":"edmonds","target":"karp","label":"Efficient computation","description":"Polynomial-time combinatorial algorithms connect to complexity classification."},{"source":"karp","target":"lipton","label":"Karp–Lipton theorem","description":"Small circuits for NP imply a collapse of the polynomial hierarchy."},{"source":"karp","target":"hopcroft","label":"Matching algorithms","description":"They developed the phased bipartite matching algorithm."},{"source":"floyd","target":"knuth","label":"Algorithm analysis","description":"Program analysis and combinatorial algorithms connect their contributions."},{"source":"pearl","target":"simon","label":"Decision and reasoning","description":"A thematic comparison of bounded rationality, uncertainty and causal models."},{"source":"knuth","target":"tarjan","label":"Data structures","description":"Detailed algorithm analysis meets structural and amortized bounds."},{"source":"cook","target":"karp","label":"NP-completeness","description":"Cook–Levin supplies the starting problem for Karp's reductions."},{"source":"cook","target":"levin","label":"Independent completeness results","description":"Their independent work established foundational completeness principles."},{"source":"hopcroft","target":"tarjan","label":"Graph algorithms","description":"Their collaborations include efficient planarity and graph-structure algorithms."},{"source":"szemeredi","target":"gowers","label":"Arithmetic progressions","description":"Gowers developed a new proof and quantitative methods using uniformity norms."},{"source":"szemeredi","target":"tao","label":"Additive structure","description":"The Green–Tao theorem uses transference beyond positive density."},{"source":"deligne","target":"wiles","label":"Arithmetic geometry","description":"A conceptual route through geometric and modular tools in number theory."},{"source":"diffie","target":"hellman","label":"Key exchange","description":"Their joint work established the public-key paradigm and key-exchange protocol."},{"source":"hellman","target":"rivest","label":"Public-key cryptography","description":"RSA is a distinct construction in the public-key paradigm."},{"source":"rivest","target":"shamir","label":"RSA","description":"They co-developed RSA with Adleman."},{"source":"shamir","target":"adleman","label":"RSA","description":"They co-developed RSA with Rivest."},{"source":"yao","target":"goldwasser","label":"Computational security","description":"Indistinguishability and simulation are central shared themes."},{"source":"thurston","target":"perelman","label":"Geometrization","description":"Perelman completed the Ricci-flow route to geometrization."},{"source":"thurston","target":"mirzakhani","label":"Geometry of surfaces","description":"Hyperbolic surfaces and moduli connect their mathematical worlds."},{"source":"levin","target":"valiant","label":"Complexity frameworks","description":"Search, counting and learning ask for different types of computational information."},{"source":"tarjan","target":"goemans","label":"Graph optimization","description":"A reading route from exact algorithms to geometric approximation."},{"source":"lovasz","target":"goemans","label":"Semidefinite optimization","description":"Theta and MAX-CUT rounding exemplify graph problems expressed by PSD matrices."},{"source":"lovasz","target":"nesetril","label":"Graph structure and limits","description":"Homomorphisms, sparsity and limit objects connect these research directions."},{"source":"valiant","target":"hastad","label":"Beyond exact decision","description":"Counting, learning and approximation expose different complexity measures."},{"source":"valiant","target":"odonnell","label":"Learning theory","description":"Fourier methods become tools for analyzing learnability."},{"source":"papadimitriou","target":"nisan","label":"Algorithmic economics","description":"Computation, strategic interaction and mechanism design form a thematic bridge."},{"source":"khachiyan","target":"goemans","label":"Convex relaxations","description":"Polynomial-time optimization supports relax-and-round algorithms."},{"source":"shamir","target":"fortnow","label":"Algebraic interactive proofs","description":"LFKN sum-check and Shamir's degree control led to IP=PSPACE."},{"source":"shamir","target":"goldwasser","label":"Interactive verification","description":"Goldwasser's foundational proof model is the setting for IP=PSPACE."},{"source":"wiles","target":"galois","label":"Galois representations","description":"A conceptual route from symmetries of equations to modern modularity methods."},{"source":"micali","target":"goldwasser","label":"Joint foundations","description":"Their collaborations include probabilistic encryption and zero knowledge."},{"source":"karmarkar","target":"lasserre","label":"Convex optimization","description":"A thematic route from interior-point methods to moment SDPs."},{"source":"goldwasser","target":"fortnow","label":"Multiple provers","description":"Interactive verification develops into the BFL characterization of MIP."},{"source":"gowers","target":"tao","label":"Uniformity and structure","description":"Higher-order analysis helps study additive patterns."},{"source":"perelman","target":"poincare","label":"Poincaré conjecture","description":"Perelman's geometrization work resolves the three-dimensional conjecture."},{"source":"mirzakhani","target":"riemann","label":"Moduli of surfaces","description":"A conceptual connection through Riemann surfaces and their parameter spaces."},{"source":"babai","target":"weisfeiler","label":"Graph isomorphism","description":"Refinement and group-theoretic methods contribute different tools."},{"source":"babai","target":"fortnow","label":"MIP=NEXP","description":"Babai, Fortnow and Lund proved the equality."},{"source":"razborov","target":"nisan","label":"Hardness and randomness","description":"Lower bounds and pseudorandom generators are linked by complexity reductions."},{"source":"nisan","target":"wigderson","label":"Nisan–Wigderson generator","description":"They developed the combinatorial-design hardness-versus-randomness construction."},{"source":"wigderson","target":"lasserre","label":"Proofs and optimization","description":"A thematic reading route into SoS and the complexity of convex relaxations."},{"source":"lipton","target":"shamir","label":"Random polynomial checking","description":"Low-degree identity tests support algebraic verification protocols."},{"source":"polya","target":"lovasz","label":"Probabilistic methods","description":"A thematic route from random walks and counting to local-dependence arguments."},{"source":"fortnow","target":"hastad","label":"PCP and approximation","description":"Multiprover ideas fed into PCP constructions and gap hardness."},{"source":"fortnow","target":"vidick","label":"Changing prover resources","description":"Compare classical MIP=NEXP with entangled MIP*=RE; the models differ."},{"source":"hastad","target":"khot","label":"Approximation thresholds","description":"Unique Games became a source of sharp conditional hardness beyond earlier PCP results."},{"source":"khot","target":"odonnell","label":"MAX-CUT hardness","description":"They coauthored the work connecting Unique Games, noise stability and MAX-CUT."},{"source":"odonnell","target":"goemans","label":"Gaussian geometry","description":"Noise-stability hardness and hyperplane rounding meet at the same constant."},{"source":"goemans","target":"raghavendra","label":"SDP approximation","description":"Raghavendra generalized the SDP-versus-hardness viewpoint to fixed CSPs."},{"source":"raghavendra","target":"lasserre","label":"Stronger relaxations","description":"A thematic comparison of basic SDP optimality and higher-level moment relaxations."},{"source":"lasserre","target":"gaifman","label":"Local and global","description":"An analogy between low-degree consistency and logical locality, not a theorem identifying them."},{"source":"weisfeiler","target":"immerman","label":"Counting logic","description":"Cai–Fürer–Immerman connects refinement limitations to bounded-variable logic."},{"source":"immerman","target":"gaifman","label":"Logical expressiveness","description":"Descriptive complexity and locality illuminate different logical resources."},{"source":"gaifman","target":"nesetril","label":"Sparse logical algorithms","description":"Locality combines with structural sparsity in model checking."}],"trails":[{"id":"limits","title":"The limits of computation","tag":"LOGIC → COMPLEXITY","description":"From axioms and universal machines to reductions and the logical description of efficient computation.","ids":["hilbert","godel","turing","cook","karp","immerman"],"field":"complexity"},{"id":"proofs","title":"From proofs to approximation","tag":"YOUR CONVERSATION THREAD","description":"How interaction and a few random checks turned into sharp limits on approximation algorithms.","ids":["goldwasser","shamir","fortnow","hastad","khot","odonnell","goemans","raghavendra","lasserre"],"field":"algorithms"},{"id":"symmetry","title":"When graphs look the same","tag":"GRAPHS → LOGIC","description":"Follow symmetry, refinement and locality into the structure of sparse graphs.","ids":["cayley","babai","weisfeiler","immerman","gaifman","nesetril"],"field":"graphs"},{"id":"randomness","title":"What randomness makes possible","tag":"PROBABILITY → ALGORITHMS","description":"From dependent random processes and existence proofs to pseudorandom generators.","ids":["markov","kolmogorov","polya","erdos","lovasz","nisan","wigderson"],"field":"analysis"},{"id":"algebra","title":"The algebra inside complexity","tag":"ALGEBRA → CIRCUITS","description":"Move from structural algebra and polynomial certificates to arithmetic circuits and proof systems.","ids":["galois","noether","hilbert","grothendieck","valiant","razborov","lasserre"],"field":"algebra"},{"id":"information","title":"Information, secrecy, trust","tag":"INFORMATION → CRYPTOGRAPHY","description":"A route through information limits, public-key exchange and proofs that protect secrets.","ids":["boole","shannon","diffie","hellman","rivest","shamir","goldwasser"],"field":"information"},{"id":"programming","title":"Giving programs a language","tag":"MACHINES → MEANING","description":"From symbolic instructions to high-level languages, invariants and formal semantics.","ids":["lovelace","turing","hopper","backus","dijkstra","hoare","scott","milner"],"field":"systems"},{"id":"space","title":"What can a space be?","tag":"GEOMETRY → TOPOLOGY","description":"See geometry expand from coordinates and curvature to manifolds, flows and moduli.","ids":["euclid","descartes","gauss","riemann","poincare","thurston","perelman","mirzakhani"],"field":"geometry"}]},"districts":[{"id":"geometry","name":"Geometry Garden","label":"Geometry","discipline":"Geometry","color":"#7F9F86","symbol":"△","question":"What can a drawing tell you before a proof begins?","description":"Follow shapes, surfaces, and spaces. Change a viewpoint and look for what survives.","x":8.878689293818311e-15,"z":-145},{"id":"analysis","name":"Continuum Meadow","label":"Analysis & probability","discipline":"Analysis & probability","color":"#BB9C64","symbol":"∫","question":"How do small changes become a pattern?","description":"Explore limits, motion, approximation, and chance through experiments you can do on paper.","x":93.20420340454821,"z":-111.07644425225182},{"id":"algebra","name":"Symmetry Court","label":"Algebra & number theory","discipline":"Algebra & number theory","color":"#A085AB","symbol":"⊕","question":"What stays the same when you change the rules?","description":"Meet equations, arithmetic, and symmetry. Try a tiny case and see which familiar rules still work.","x":142.79712418677016,"z":-25.1789857617049},{"id":"logic","name":"Logic Grove","label":"Logic & foundations","discipline":"Logic & foundations","color":"#7B9EB0","symbol":"∀","question":"What exactly follows from your assumptions?","description":"Slow down a claim, inspect its quantifiers, and build examples that separate nearby statements.","x":125.57368354874362,"z":72.49999999999997},{"id":"systems","name":"Systems Orchard","label":"Programming & databases","discipline":"Programming & databases","color":"#6FA89A","symbol":"{ }","question":"How do simple rules make a reliable system?","description":"Give ideas a structure: records, instructions, languages, and checks that make them usable.","x":49.59292078222198,"z":136.2554300139567},{"id":"information","name":"Signal Gardens","label":"Information & computation","discipline":"Information & computation","color":"#BD8C73","symbol":"01","question":"How much of a message do you really need?","description":"Investigate codes, signals, inference, and secrecy by making and testing a small message.","x":-49.59292078222197,"z":136.25543001395673},{"id":"complexity","name":"Possibility Woods","label":"Computational complexity","discipline":"Computational complexity","color":"#8888AE","symbol":"P?","question":"What makes a problem easy to state and hard to solve?","description":"Compare finding, checking, and searching. Keep track of how a method grows with its input.","x":-125.57368354874357,"z":72.50000000000004},{"id":"graphs","name":"Bridge Grove","label":"Graphs & combinatorics","discipline":"Graphs & combinatorics","color":"#B68A98","symbol":"⋈","question":"Which connections hold a whole world together?","description":"Draw networks, count possibilities, and change one edge to discover what depends on it.","x":-142.7971241867702,"z":-25.178985761704855},{"id":"algorithms","name":"Pathfinder Terrace","label":"Algorithms & optimization","discipline":"Algorithms & optimization","color":"#93A36B","symbol":"→","question":"What is the next step, and why is it the right one?","description":"Walk through a procedure by hand. Watch its choices, its memory, and the reason it succeeds.","x":-93.20420340454822,"z":-111.07644425225179}],"missions":[{"id":"circle-estimate","district":"geometry","title":"Measure a curve with straight lines","minutes":15,"prompt":"How closely can a polygon approximate a circle?","steps":["Draw a circle of radius 3 cm and mark six equally spaced points. Join them to make an inscribed regular hexagon.","Split the hexagon into six equilateral triangles. Use triangle area = √3 × side² / 4 to calculate its total area.","Compare with the circle area, 9π cm². Sketch twelve equally spaced points and explain why the new inscribed polygon has more area."],"output":"A drawing, a numerical comparison, and one sentence about improving the approximation."},{"id":"area-by-slices","district":"analysis","title":"Watch an approximation settle","minutes":15,"prompt":"Estimate the area under y = x² between x = 0 and x = 1.","steps":["Divide [0, 1] into two equal intervals. For each interval, multiply its width by the function value at its midpoint and add the results.","Repeat with four equal intervals, then eight. Record the three estimates in a small table.","Compare each estimate with the exact area 1/3. Draw the midpoint rectangles and describe which part of the curve each misses."],"output":"Three estimates and a picture that explains their error."},{"id":"modular-roots","district":"algebra","title":"An equation with unexpected roots","minutes":15,"prompt":"Does x² = 1 always have just two solutions?","steps":["For the remainders 0 through 4, compute x² modulo 5 and list the solutions of x² ≡ 1 (mod 5).","Do the same for remainders modulo 8, then modulo 9. Keep each multiplication visible.","For the solution x = 3 modulo 8, calculate (x − 1)(x + 1). Notice that the product is zero modulo 8 even though neither factor is zero. Write the arithmetic rule that fails here."],"output":"Three short tables and a concrete example showing that a zero product need not have a zero factor modulo 8."},{"id":"implication-cards","district":"logic","title":"Turn a claim around","minutes":15,"prompt":"Separate an implication, its converse, and its contrapositive.","steps":["Write the claim: “If an integer is divisible by 4, then it is even.” Identify its assumption and its conclusion.","Write its converse and its contrapositive. Test each statement on the integers 2, 4, 5, and 8.","Build the four-row truth table for P ⇒ Q, Q ⇒ P, and ¬Q ⇒ ¬P. Compare the columns."],"output":"Three precise statements, a counterexample to the converse, and a truth table."},{"id":"tiny-database","district":"systems","title":"Make a tiny library work","minutes":15,"prompt":"Design a reading log that can answer a useful question.","steps":["Create a Books table with id, title, and author. Add three books. Create a Sessions table with book_id, date, and minutes; add five reading sessions for just two of the books.","Join each session to its book using the matching id. Sum reading minutes for each book by hand.","Write precise pseudocode or a SQL query to produce all three totals, including zero for the unread book. SQL hint: use LEFT JOIN, GROUP BY, and COALESCE(SUM(minutes), 0)."],"output":"Two tiny tables and a repeatable way to find the minutes spent on each book."},{"id":"shorter-message","district":"information","title":"Build a code you can decode","minutes":15,"prompt":"Can a common letter use less space than a rare one?","steps":["Take the message AAAABBCD. Encode A, B, C, D with the fixed two-bit codes 00, 01, 10, 11 and count the bits.","Encode the same message with A = 0, B = 10, C = 110, D = 111. Count the bits and decode your result from left to right.","Explain why no codeword is the beginning of another. Try DDDD and compare the lengths again; account for the codebook separately if you would transmit it."],"output":"Two encoded messages and an example showing when the variable-length code helps."},{"id":"greedy-counterexample","district":"complexity","title":"A tempting choice goes wrong","minutes":15,"prompt":"Test a greedy rule for the 0–1 knapsack problem: each item is indivisible.","steps":["Set capacity to 50. Use three items with (weight, value) equal to (10, 60), (20, 100), and (30, 120). Calculate each value-to-weight ratio.","Take items in decreasing ratio order whenever they fit. Record the final weight and value.","List all eight subsets, discard those over capacity, and find the best remaining value. State exactly which greedy rule your example disproves."],"output":"A complete small counterexample, including the greedy and optimal choices."},{"id":"bridge-experiment","district":"graphs","title":"One edge changes everything","minutes":15,"prompt":"Find the edges that hold a network together.","steps":["Draw two disjoint four-cycles. Join one vertex of the first to one vertex of the second with a single edge.","For every edge, imagine deleting it while keeping all vertices. Mark it as a bridge precisely when the graph becomes disconnected.","Add a second edge between a different pair of vertices across the two cycles. Repeat the deletion test and explain what happened to the original bridge."],"output":"Two annotated graphs and an explanation using an alternative path."},{"id":"shortest-path-walk","district":"algorithms","title":"Find a route one choice at a time","minutes":15,"prompt":"Run Dijkstra’s algorithm by hand on a small undirected weighted graph.","steps":["Draw vertices A through F and edges AB:4, AC:1, CB:2, BD:1, CD:5, DE:3, CF:7, and FE:1. Start from A.","Set A’s tentative distance to zero and all others to infinity. Repeatedly settle the unsettled vertex with the smallest distance. For each unsettled neighbor, keep the smaller of its old distance and the settled distance plus the connecting edge’s weight.","Record which predecessor gave each improvement and reconstruct one shortest path from A to E. Consider how a later negative edge could undermine a distance you already settled."],"output":"A distance table, a shortest path, and one sentence explaining the crucial assumption."},{"id":"draw-a-connection","district":"general","title":"Draw an idea before naming it","minutes":15,"prompt":"Make a small abstract drawing about connection.","steps":["Choose five points on a page. Connect some pairs with red curves and some pairs with blue curves. Keep a small record of which pairs are connected.","Redraw the same connections with the points in different positions. Let the new drawing have a different mood.","Give each drawing a title. Write one sentence describing what changed visually and one describing what stayed the same mathematically."],"output":"Two drawings of the same colored graph with different visual personalities."},{"id":"three-shot-question","district":"general","title":"A question in three quiet shots","minutes":15,"prompt":"Plan a tiny film about noticing something you usually pass by.","steps":["Choose an ordinary object or place within reach: a window, an empty chair, a cup, or a doorway. Write one question it suggests.","Sketch three shots: the setting, one revealing detail, and the setting again. Give each a duration and one sound; no dialogue is needed.","Record the shots with your phone or keep the storyboard. Describe how the final shot changes what the first shot meant."],"output":"A three-frame storyboard or a very short film with one clear question."},{"id":"modular-rhythm","district":"general","title":"Hear two clocks meet","minutes":15,"prompt":"Make a rhythm from two simple repeating rules.","steps":["Write beats 1 through 12. Put a red mark on beats divisible by 3 and a blue mark on beats divisible by 4.","Tap a different surface for each color, or draw a score. Move slowly through the twelve beats, combining the taps when both marks appear.","Repeat with periods 3 and 5 across fifteen beats. Predict the next shared beat for each pattern and connect your prediction to the least common multiple."],"output":"Two short rhythm scores and a rule for when their accents meet."}],"landmarks":{"fountain":{"name":"Question Fountain","title":"Begin with a question","description":"A place to pause, choose a small experiment, and let curiosity decide your next walk.","missionIds":["bridge-experiment","greedy-counterexample","implication-cards"]},"lake":{"name":"Reflection Lake","title":"Let an idea settle","description":"Watch a pattern change slowly. Draw what you see and compare it with what you expected.","missionIds":["area-by-slices","circle-estimate","modular-rhythm"]},"studio":{"name":"Open Studio","title":"Leave with something you made","description":"Turn a connection into a drawing, a rhythm, or a few quiet frames. A small finished piece is enough.","missionIds":["draw-a-connection","three-shot-question","modular-rhythm"]},"amphitheater":{"name":"Conversation Amphitheater","title":"Make an idea clear enough to share","description":"Explain a tiny example, invite a counterexample, and discover where your assumptions enter.","missionIds":["implication-cards","shorter-message","bridge-experiment"]},"observatory":{"name":"Possibility Observatory","title":"Look beyond the first answer","description":"Inspect the landscape of possibilities. Test a rule, trace a method, and write the next question.","missionIds":["greedy-counterexample","modular-roots","shortest-path-walk"]}}};
